  - [1mConsole only, structured[0m — the shared module formats levels and context; in the browser it just calls [38;5;153mconsole.*[39m.
    Buys: zero dependencies, zero transport, ships today. Costs: you only see logs when someone hands you their devtools
    output — which doesn't really answer "debug production issues." Trivially upgraded later.
  - [1mBuffer + manual export[0m — keep a ring buffer in memory, expose something like [38;5;153mgetLogs()[39m so a user (or a support flow)
    can copy them out on request. Buys: real session history, still no network, no privacy surface beyond the page.
    Costs: needs a human in the loop to retrieve them. Cheap to change.
  - [1mPOST to your own endpoint[0m — batch and ship to a collector you run. Buys: actual production visibility, unattended.
    Costs: you need that endpoint (it doesn't exist in this repo), plus batching, failure/retry, and a firm redaction
    rule. This is the expensive, hard-to-reverse one.
  - [1mThird-party service[0m (Sentry or similar) — buys the collector and a UI. Costs: a runtime dependency, an account, and
    login-flow data going to a vendor.

  [1mMy recommendation: buffer + manual export[0m, with the module's sink as a documented seam so a POST sink drops in later
  without touching a single call site. It's the most debugging value available without inventing infrastructure this
  repo doesn't have, and it doesn't commit you to a vendor before you know what the failures look like.

  One thing I'm flagging now rather than after: [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m currently logs the username, and it sits next to a password
  field. Whatever we pick, the shared module needs an explicit rule about credentials — I'll bring that back as a design
  section, but if you choose a network sink it stops being a detail and becomes a blocker I'd want settled before any
  code.
